+*   **When not to use:** For real-time applications with very large volumes on low-end hardware, or when surface geometry is sufficient and preferred [S2, unofficial][S28][S43, unofficial][S44, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Volume Ray Casting was introduced in the mid-1980s as a fundamental technique for direct volume rendering. Key early papers include Tuy and Tuy (1984) and Levoy (1988) [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of surface-based rendering (like isosurfacing) which required thresholding and lost internal data [S1, S4, S12, S22, unofficial][S26, S43]. The method is based on the volume rendering integral, which models the interaction of light with participating media [S24, unofficial][S30]. It became widely adopted with the advent of GPU hardware, which allowed for the parallel execution of ray marching in fragment shaders [S6, S13, S16, S27, unofficial][S30][S35, S41, S45, S49, unofficial].
+Volume Ray Casting was introduced in the mid-1980s as a fundamental technique for direct volume rendering. Key early papers include Tuy and Tuy (1984) and Levoy (1988) [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of surface-based rendering (like isosurfacing) which required thresholding and lost internal data [S1, S4, S12, S22, unofficial][S26, S43, unofficial]. The method is based on the volume rendering integral, which models the interaction of light with participating media [S24, unofficial][S30, unofficial]. It became widely adopted with the advent of GPU hardware, which allowed for the parallel execution of ray marching in fragment shaders [S6, S13, S16, S27, unofficial][S30, unofficial][S35, unofficial][S41, S45][S49, unofficial].
 
